# Line endings: LF in the repository, and LF when checked out, on every platform.
#
# Most of what is in Artie ends up in a Linux container or on an embedded Linux image, where
# a CRLF breaks shell scripts, Dockerfiles and config files. This overrides a Windows
# machine's core.autocrlf, so a checkout there looks the same as one on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep Windows line endings.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary files. Git detects these on its own; listing them makes sure it never guesses wrong.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.tgz  binary
*.fzz  binary
*.stl  binary
*.uf2  binary
*.bin  binary
*.elf  binary
